      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Metabolic syndrome (MetS) is a constellation of interrelated risk factors of metabolic origin. Some studies suggest a possible link between low total testosterone (TT) levels and the presence of MetS."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To analyze the strength and independence of associations between TT and MetS components in non-diabetic men."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "In this cross-sectional study, 143 non-diabetic men older than 40 were analyzed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOMES MEASURE",
          "content": "Blood samples were collected to evaluate metabolic profile and TT levels. MetS was defined as the presence of three or more of the following characteristics: fasting blood glucose levels\u2009≥\u2009100\u2009mg/dL, triglyceride\u2009≥\u2009150\u2009mg/dL, HDL-c\u2009<\u200940\u2009mg/dL, hypertension or blood pressure\u2009≥\u2009130/85\u2009mmHg, and waist girth\u2009>\u2009102\u2009cm."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Mean age of the study population was 61.5\u2009±\u20098.61 years old. MetS was present in 47.9% of the individuals. Thirty-four men had low TT and MetS was observed in 23 (70%) against 50 (46%) in those with normal TT (≥\u2009300\u2009ng/dL) (OR 4.94, p\u2009<\u20090.01), adjusted to confounder's factors. In multiple linear regression analysis, only waist circumference (Beta: -0.395; p\u2009=\u20090.03) and HDL-c (Beta: 0.19; p\u2009=\u20090.04) remained significantly correlated with TT levels."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Low TT levels were associated with MetS diagnosis. Abdominal obesity was the MetS component independently correlated to low TT levels."
        }
      ]},
